"The antipode of latitude φ and longitude λ is latitude −φ and longitude λ ± {#}°. Its horizon lies in the same plane as yours (the Earth's {#} {#} km is nothing next to the stars), but its zenith points where your nadir does: the half of the sky below your horizon is the half above theirs. A star at altitude h and azimuth A here is at −h and {#}° − A there, so when your Sun is up, theirs is down by the same angle: noon here is midnight there, and the season is the opposite one. The Moon is close enough that each of you sees it shifted toward the horizon by up to {#}° (its parallax), so the two altitudes add to as much as −{#}° instead of {#}. Clocks there run {#} hours apart, and the date may differ by one. Most of the Earth's land has ocean at its antipode.",
