"Directions are worked out as vectors. The Earth turns by the Earth rotation angle of the IERS Conventions ({#}), measured from the Celestial Intermediate Origin, which the demo carries along the moving pole; the pole and the ecliptic move by the long term precession of Vondrák, Capitaine and Wallace ({#}), valid for {#} {#} years either side of {#}. Nutation (up to {#}″) is left out, and so are the aberration of starlight for the stars (up to {#}″; the Sun's place includes it) and refraction, which lifts things at the horizon by about half a degree in the real sky: here the Sun sets when its center reaches the horizon. Star places are the catalog's J2000 positions moved along their proper motions (no radial velocity). The Sun is from Meeus ch. {#} (about {#}°), the Moon from Meeus ch. {#} (about {#}″), seen from your place on the Earth, which matters for eclipses. Eclipses compare the two disks as seen from your place (solar) or the Moon with the Earth's shadow, its radii from the Explanatory Supplement ({#}) (lunar); the Moon is taken as a smooth sphere, so contact times agree with NASA's predictions to within about a minute: totality at Dallas on April {#}, {#} runs from {#}:{#}:{#} to {#}:{#}:{#} UT here and from {#}:{#}:{#} to {#}:{#}:{#} UT in NASA's tables. Both are fits made for dates within a few thousand years of {#}, so far from today they are approximate; precession and the stars do not depend on them. Times are standard time for the time zone nearest your longitude (longitude/{#}, rounded), with no daylight saving; ΔT = TT − UT from Espenak and Meeus ({#}), within about {#} s of the measured value today and an estimate in the far past and future. Dates use the Gregorian calendar extended back in time, with year {#} for {#} BC. The pictures: the horizon view is a stereographic projection (shapes kept, sizes growing toward the edges), the whole sky is an azimuthal equidistant map with the zenith at the center, and the star chart plots right ascension against declination of date. Star dots grow with brightness; the Sun and Moon are drawn larger than life except in the eclipse inset, which is to scale.",
